  "generated_text": "To solve the problem correctly, let's carefully review the steps and ensure all calculations are accurate.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the units digits of the powers of 3:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 & = 3 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 3) \\\\\n3^2 & = 9 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 9) \\\\\n3^3 & = 27 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 7) \\\\\n3^4 & = 81 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 1) \\\\\n3^5 & = 243 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 3) \\\\\n3^6 & = 729 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 9) \\\\\n3^7 & = 2187 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 7) \\\\\n3^8 & = 6561 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 1) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. The sequence of units digits for the terms \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots\\) is thus 3, 9, 7, 1.\n\nNext, we need to count the number of terms in the series \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 2\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(n\\) such that \\(2n - 1 = 2009\\):\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 2009 \\implies 2n = 2010 \\implies n = 1005\n\\]\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the series.\n\nNow, we need to determine how many complete groups of 4 terms are there in 1005 terms:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1005}{4} \\right\\rfloor = 251 \\text{ complete groups}\n\\]\nEach complete group of 4 terms sums to:\n\\[\n3 + 9 + 7 + 1 = 20\n\\]\nThus, the sum of the units digits of 251 complete groups is:\n\\[\n251 \\times 20 = 5020\n\\]\nThe units digit of 5020 is 0.\n\nNext, we need to add the units digit of the remaining term, which is the first term of the next group:\n\\[\n3\n\\]\nThe units digit of 3 is 3.\n\nFinally, we sum the units digits:\n\\[\n0 + 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
