      "content": "What is the units digit of $3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}$?\n\nThink step by step inside <think> </think> tags, then give your final answer inside \\boxed{}."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we need to analyze the units digits of the individual terms in the series and then sum them up.\n\nFirst, let's observe the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 3:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 & = 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^2 & = 9 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^3 & = 27 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^4 & = 81 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n3^5 & = 243 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n3^6 & = 729 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n3^7 & = 2187 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n3^8 & = 6561 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, the units digits of \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots\\) form a repeating sequence with a period of 4.\n\nNext, we need to count how many terms are in the series \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\). This is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by:\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(n\\) such that \\(2n - 1 = 2009\\):\n\\[\n2n - 1 = 2009 \\implies 2n = 2010 \\implies n = 1005\n\\]\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the series.\n\nNow, we need to sum the units digits of these 1005 terms. Since the units digits repeat every 4 terms, we can group the terms into sets of 4:\n\\[\n(3, 9, 7, 1), (3, 9, 7, 1), \\ldots, (3, 9, 7, 1)\n\\]\nThere are \\(1005 / 4 = 251\\) complete groups of 4 terms, and 1 additional term (since \\(1005 \\mod 4 = 1\\)).\n\nThe sum of the units digits in one complete group of 4 terms is:\n\\[\n3 + 9 + 7 + 1 = 20\n\\]\nThus, the sum of the units digits of 251 complete groups is:\n\\[\n251 \\times 20 = 5020\n\\]\nThe units digit of 5020 is 0.\n\nNow, we need to add the units digit of the remaining term, which is the first term of the next group:\n\\[\n3\n\\]\nThe units digit of 3 is 3.\n\nFinally, we sum the units digits:\n\\[\n0 + 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
